<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Douglass is a 34-year-old editor and public speaker with post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the right hand who comes in the same day because his voice has been hoarse for three days. A cold with a runny nose and a mild sore throat began six days ago, and over the next four days he gave three long talks without a microphone. Since then his voice has been rough and breaks when he raises it, and it tires by evening. He has a mild tickling cough with no sputum and no blood. He has had no fever above 37.5 °C, no trouble or pain swallowing, no noisy breathing, no shortness of breath, and no heartburn. He has never smoked. He has two more talks next week and wants to know whether he should give them. He uses the diclofenac gel on his hand most days and takes no other medicine. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago, after a cold that began six days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Voice box</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Three days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Rough voice that breaks when raised and tires by evening</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; can talk, but not at length</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Worse by evening</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Three long talks without a microphone during a cold</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better with rest and warm drinks</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Mild dry cough; no fever, trouble swallowing, noisy breathing, or blood</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

