    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral laryngitis (J04.0)</content>. Hoarseness after a cold and heavy voice use, without fever, swallowing difficulty, stridor, or red flags in a man who has never smoked. This is viral and self-limited; antibiotics would not help. Laryngoscopy is not needed now, because the hoarseness is short, follows a clear cause, and has no warning features. [S]<list><item>Voice rest for three or four days: speak softly and briefly, and do not whisper, which strains the voice more.</item><item>Warm fluids, humidified air, and throat lozenges as needed; no antibiotics.</item><item>Postpone next week&apos;s two talks if his voice has not recovered, and use a microphone when he returns to speaking.</item><item>Return if the hoarseness lasts beyond four weeks for laryngoscopy, or at once for trouble breathing, noisy breathing, trouble swallowing, or coughing blood.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the right hand (M19.141)</content>. Stable on diclofenac gel and home exercises; the gel has no meaningful interaction with this illness. [S]<list><item>Continue as before.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016); Clinical Practice Guideline: Hoarseness (Dysphonia) (Update) (2018)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Call or message if the voice is not improving in a week. Planned five-month review in August. Next scheduled visit 1852-08-18: five-month review of the hand, sleep, and trauma-related symptoms. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
